<h2>Buttons</h2>
<hr>
<p>Buttons are some of the most common UI components used in pk-gov, providing a wide range of functions
  from navigating through forms to returning users to the previous page. As a result, there are several
  different types of buttons available.
</p>
<h3>"True" buttons</h3>
<button class='button' type='button'>This is a "True" button</button>
<p>These are created with the HTML button element, with styles and hover animations applied when the
  <code>'button'</code> class is added. "True" buttons do nothing by default, so functionality must be 
  added with JavaScript. If a button is meant to redirect users to different pages or otherwise behave 
  like links, the "Link" button should be used instead.
</p>
<h3>"Link buttons</h3>
<a class='button' href=''>This "Link" button refreshes the page</a>
<p>These are created with HTML anchor elements, with styles and hover animations applied when the <code>'return'</code>,
  <code>'download'</code> or <code>'button'</code> class is added. "Link" buttons have the same functionality as
  regular anchor elements, and should be used for linking to different pages or downloadable content. Buttons that
  do not provide either of these functions should be "True" buttons.
</p>

<h2>Button rows</h2>
<hr>
<div class='end-buttons'>
  <button class='button' type='button'>This is a "True" button</button>
  <a class='button' href=''>This "Link" button refreshes the page</a>
</div>
<p>By default, buttons are treated as block elements. If multiple buttons need to be placed in a single row,
  the buttons should be placed in a container with the <code>'start-buttons'</code> or <code>'end-buttons'</code> classes:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>'start-buttons'</code> aligns buttons from left to right, such as the buttons in the PIBSS Start Page.</li>
  <li><code>'end-buttons'</code> aligns buttons in the centre as shown in the example above.</li>
</ul>

<h2>'Utility' buttons</h2>
<hr>
<p>These buttons appear on a wide range of pages, with their styles and behaviour automatically applied when
  the specified class is added onto the element. These buttons do not need to be manually added into pages in
  normal situations, as these buttons are contained in relevant page templates.
</p>
<h3>Print button</h3>
<button type="button" class="print">Print page</button>
<p>This button enables users to print the chosen page in a print format. Styles are altered to better suit
  physical paper, with elements that are interactive or irrelevant in a printed medium being removed. It is
  a "true" button with the <code>'print'</code> class added onto it.
</p>
<h3>Share button</h3>
<button type="button" class="share">Share page</button>
<p>This button opens the user's share menu to allow them to share the current page with others. It is a "true"
  button with the <code>'share'</code> class added onto it.
</p>

<h2>Table of Contents</h2>
<hr>
<p>The table of contents or 'In this page:' section is automatically created for content pages such as news articles,
  and information pages, including this page. It is created by detecting all headings (h1-6) present on the page when
  running pk-gov's build script, and jumps to the heading a user clicks on.
</p>

<h2>Cards</h2>
<hr>
<p>Cards are mainly used on 'homepages' to link to other content pages. Cards contain a title and description
  as their content. The basic structure of a card is as shown below, with styles and hover animations applied
  when all classes shown below are added:
</p>
<pre>
  &lt;div class="card"&gt;
    &lt;div class='card-text'&gt;
      &lt;h2 class="card-title"&gt;&lt;a class='card-link' href=''&gt;Card title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
      &lt;p class="card-description"&gt;Describe the content of this card.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class='card-arrow'&gt;>&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
</pre>
<p>Cards MUST be contained within a container with the <code>'all-cards'</code> class. Therefore, cards should
  only be used when there There are two width
  options for cards, the regular width (applied by default) and a wider width when the <code>wide-cards</code>
  class is added, as shown below (NOTE: All cards refresh the page when clicked):
</p>
